  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe upper level ridge over much of the western and central US will \ncontinue to bring warming and drying conditions to the Inland \nNorthwest. The ridge will be at its strongest today (Sunday), and as \nsuch, temperatures today and tomorrow will be the highest of the \nweek. High temperatures will be in the mid to high 70s, with some \nplaces in the deeper basin areas reaching 80. These temperatures are \nnearly 10 to 12 degrees above normal for this time of year. Low \ntemperatures are around 4 to 6 degrees above normal. On Monday, a \nweak disturbance will pass through, flattening the ridge and raising \nafternoon wind gusts to 15-20 mph through much of the forecast area. \nTemperatures will drop slightly between Tuesday and Thursday, but \nnot much, still remaining unseasonably warm. \n\nConditions remain this way until Friday, when long term models show \na robust cold front moving through the region. While there is little \nagreement in overall track and timing, ensembles show good agreement \nin a steep drop in temperatures from Friday to Saturday, nearly 10 \nto 12 degrees. This will bring temperatures to right around normal \nfor this time of year, with highs in the low to mid 60s and lows in \nmid to high 30s (lower 30s across sheltered valleys). Additionally, \nthere is agreement on gusty winds accompanying the low, with overall \ngusts 15 to 25 mph and gusts 30 to 35 mph possible in the basin and \nWaterville Plateau areas. While there is a chance for precipitation, \noverall chances remain low, generally 30 percent or less in the \nmountains and even lower elsewhere. Overall, expect a beautiful, \nwarm fall week across the Pacific Northwest through Thursday, then \ncooler and unsettled weather Friday into next weekend. /AS",
